\section{Introduction}
Fiwaga (ISO 639-3 fiw; also Fimaga, Fiwage) is a Papuan language of Southern Highlands Province, Papua New Guinea, in the East Kutubuan group with Foi. A cited speaker count is 300 (a 1981 source); Joshua Project lists 1,000--1,100 people. It is among the least documented languages of the region.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes were found in the searches performed.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Fiwaga language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiwaga_language}
\item Wikipedia, East Kutubuan languages: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Kutubuan_languages}
\item New Guinea World, Lake Kutubu languages: \url{https://newguineaworld.linguistik.uzh.ch/families/papuan-gulf/kikori-river/lake-kutubu/start}
\item Grambank entry (Foi, related language): \url{https://grambank.clld.org/languages/foii1241}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project language profile: the written form is essentially undeveloped; Bible translation has begun but is far from complete: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/languages/fiw}
\item Joshua Project people-group profile: \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/11215/PP}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
No audio, video or software resources were found. Joshua Project reports no audio Bible or Jesus Film.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Documentation is very thin and no orthography or teaching material was found. Any curriculum would first require community-led orthography and materials development, with fieldwork to establish the current status of the language.
